posts.body:
Thanks! That's working a lot better. The freezing bug was an issue with I am guessing an interaction with another plugin, Ignis Audio Synchronizer. I had it turning on in the event chain, to sync the first intro story dialogue box with the music starting. This made it freeze. When I deleted the synchronizer from turning on, there is a very slight delay where the dialogue begins to show up, and *then* the music starts. But, it doesn't freeze anymore.
I should be able to do more testing tomorrow. Once I know it's all working, I'll be sure to come back and post how to get it as a toggleable option in the menu. Though, it will be for if someone is using VisuStella Options Core.
EDIT: Okay, so to get this as a toggleable option in VisuStella Options core... and this is assuming you're using subfolder names through this audio swap plugin... Follow the order of screenshots to get to where you can make a new option (window 4). Copy and paste one of the existing ones in the list, I dragged it to the top after.
Now doubleclick the option you just made, you will get the following window. Where it says "symbol" you just name it something for yourself to identify what it is. Where it says STR: Text is where you put what you want it to be called in the menu.
Now in this option parameters, scroll down to Function where it says Draw Option. Open that and scroll down until you see this:
const off is for the left setting, const on is for the right setting. As you can see I named off as 8-bit and on as Instrumental, for my 2 different soundtracks.
Now go back to the parameters list (where it had said Function before), and scroll down to JS: Cursor Right. Open that and scroll down to where it says // Perform Actions You need to update this section to look something like this:
// Perform Actions
this.setConfigValue(symbol, true);
this.redrawItem(this.findSymbol(symbol));
if (this.getConfigValue(symbol) !== lastValue) {
this.playCursorSound();
PluginManager.callCommand(this, 'Lyra_AltAudio', 'ChangeSubByName', { name: 'instrumental' });
this.playCursorSound();
}
Where it says instrumental, name it what you want the right setting in the option to be.
Now go back to the parameter list (where it had said Function before) and go to JS: Cursor Left. Now add that same code to Perform Actions, but where it says name: 'instrumental', change instrumental to what you want the left option to be named.
Now OK all the way out and you should have an option in the settings.
You can also let the player choose the soundtrack through an event command, ie. do a "Show Choices" event that does a plugin command for each choice to use Lyra_AltAudio to look at ie. SubFolder name to change to that soundtrack. This doesn't seem to update the choice in the option that I just showed how to add. But I have this option as a dialogue choice at the start of my game, to at least make the player aware that there are 2 soundtracks.
Let me know if anything doesn't seem to make sense and I'll try to update/add more detail while the info is still fresh.

Thanks for your help in testing!
On my end it always changed the music on the options screen, whether it was from the main menu or mid game, so not sure what is causing that. At least it is using the default options menu addition that I added.
Unfortunately since Visustella's code is obfuscated (I can't just open it up and see what it does) I can't do much more than what I've done. If you're using Visustella's Options Core than the best bet is to disable my menu option and add the option yourself in the Options Core settings.
Building off your tutorial of adding it as an option in Options Core a few posts ago, I've found a few tweaks that might help:
add your option to the Audio category like normal and drag it wherever in the list you want it.
Then open it up and set
Icon
and
STR: Text
to whatever you want the option to appear as.
Then open up
JS: Draw
Option, scroll to the bottom and look for the section //Draw Status Text. Replace that with:
// Draw Status Text
const curost = LyraVultur.AltAudio.GetNameFromId(LyraVultur.AltAudio.curid - 1);
this.changePaintOpacity(true);
this.drawText(curost, rect.x + halfWidth, rect.y, quarterWidth, "center");
Next open up
JS: Process OK
and erase everything under // Perform Actions.
Then open up
JS: Cursor Right
and find the // Perform Actions section. Replace it with:
// Perform Actions
LyraVultur.AltAudio.SetAltNext();
this.redrawItem(this.findSymbol(symbol));
this.playCursorSound();
Then do the same for
JS: Cursor Left
, but change the code to:
// Perform Actions
LyraVultur.AltAudio.SetAltPrev();
this.redrawItem(this.findSymbol(symbol));
this.playCursorSound();
Try that out and it should work!
